WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.046 --> 00:00.256 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

00:00.726 --> 00:01.486 align:middle
Good evening, everyone.

00:01.936 --> 00:04.726 align:middle
Today the Federal Reserve
took a number of actions

00:04.726 --> 00:08.196 align:middle
to support American families and
business and the economy overall

00:08.796 --> 00:10.256 align:middle
and to promote the
flow of credit

00:10.256 --> 00:12.916 align:middle
as we weather disruptions
caused by the coronavirus.

00:14.156 --> 00:16.656 align:middle
The virus is having a
profound effect on people

00:16.656 --> 00:18.576 align:middle
across the United States
and around the world.

00:19.566 --> 00:21.866 align:middle
On behalf of my colleagues
at the Federal Reserve,

00:22.106 --> 00:24.626 align:middle
our first concern is for
those who've been harmed.

00:26.326 --> 00:29.396 align:middle
Families, businesses, schools,
organizations, and governments

00:29.396 --> 00:32.306 align:middle
at all levels are taking steps
to protect people's health.

00:33.606 --> 00:35.196 align:middle
These measures, which
are essential

00:35.196 --> 00:36.386 align:middle
for containing the outbreak,

00:36.686 --> 00:38.786 align:middle
will nonetheless
understandably take a toll

00:38.786 --> 00:40.636 align:middle
on economic activity
in the near term.

00:42.396 --> 00:44.646 align:middle
While the primary response
to this challenge will come

00:44.716 --> 00:47.226 align:middle
from our health-care
providers and policy experts,

00:48.126 --> 00:50.856 align:middle
economic policymakers
must do what we can

00:50.856 --> 00:53.806 align:middle
to ease hardship caused by
the disruptions to the economy

00:54.856 --> 00:58.196 align:middle
and to support a swift return
to normal once they have passed.

01:00.336 --> 01:02.866 align:middle
The Federal Reserve's role
is guided by our mandate

01:02.866 --> 01:06.176 align:middle
from Congress to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

01:06.176 --> 01:09.416 align:middle
for the American people, along
with our responsibilities

01:09.416 --> 01:11.546 align:middle
to promote the stability
of the financial system.

01:12.786 --> 01:14.366 align:middle
Today we reduced
the target range

01:14.366 --> 01:17.176 align:middle
for our policy interest
rate by 1 percentage point,

01:17.836 --> 01:20.576 align:middle
bringing it close to zero,
and said that we expect

01:20.576 --> 01:23.836 align:middle
to maintain the rate at this
level until we're confident

01:23.836 --> 01:26.926 align:middle
that the economy has weathered
recent events and is on track

01:26.986 --> 01:28.506 align:middle
to achieve our maximum
employment

01:28.566 --> 01:29.856 align:middle
and price stability goals.

01:31.036 --> 01:33.676 align:middle
In addition, we took other
actions to support the flow

01:33.676 --> 01:38.086 align:middle
of credit to households
and businesses.

01:38.966 --> 01:40.806 align:middle
Before describing our
actions more fully,

01:40.906 --> 01:42.276 align:middle
I will share how my colleagues

01:42.276 --> 01:44.716 align:middle
and I currently view
the economic outlook.

01:45.036 --> 01:49.416 align:middle
The economy came into
this challenging period

01:49.416 --> 01:50.306 align:middle
on a strong footing.

01:51.396 --> 01:54.926 align:middle
The unemployment rate was 3.5
percent in February and has been

01:54.926 --> 01:58.126 align:middle
at or near half-century
lows for almost two years.

01:58.986 --> 02:01.066 align:middle
Job gains have been
running at a solid pace,

02:01.246 --> 02:04.296 align:middle
well above what is needed to
provide jobs for new entrants

02:04.346 --> 02:05.256 align:middle
into the labor market.

02:06.826 --> 02:08.796 align:middle
Participation in the
labor force by people

02:08.796 --> 02:11.686 align:middle
in their prime working years
remained near its highest rate

02:11.686 --> 02:12.686 align:middle
in more than a decade.

02:12.796 --> 02:15.536 align:middle
And wages have been
rising, particularly

02:15.536 --> 02:16.836 align:middle
for lower-paying jobs.

02:18.076 --> 02:20.496 align:middle
Overall economic activity
has been expanding

02:20.496 --> 02:23.366 align:middle
at a moderate rate, even
though weak growth abroad

02:23.366 --> 02:25.876 align:middle
and trade developments have
been weighing on some sectors.

02:26.826 --> 02:30.866 align:middle
U.S. banks are strong, have high
levels of capital and liquidity,

02:31.106 --> 02:32.846 align:middle
and are well positioned
to provide credit

02:32.846 --> 02:34.266 align:middle
to households and businesses.

02:34.626 --> 02:37.306 align:middle
Against this favorable backdrop,

02:37.656 --> 02:40.516 align:middle
the virus presents
significant economic challenges.

02:40.996 --> 02:42.866 align:middle
Like others, we expect
that the illness

02:42.866 --> 02:44.606 align:middle
and the measures now
being put in place

02:44.606 --> 02:47.786 align:middle
to stem its spread will
have a significant effect

02:47.786 --> 02:49.756 align:middle
on economic activity
in the near term.

02:50.876 --> 02:52.336 align:middle
Those in travel, tourism,

02:52.336 --> 02:56.056 align:middle
and hospitality industries are
already seeing a sharp drop

02:56.056 --> 02:56.646 align:middle
in business.

02:57.666 --> 02:59.096 align:middle
In addition, the effects

02:59.096 --> 03:01.636 align:middle
of the outbreak are
restraining economic activity

03:01.636 --> 03:04.946 align:middle
in many foreign economies,
which is causing difficulties

03:04.946 --> 03:07.846 align:middle
for U.S. industries that
rely on global supply chains.

03:09.306 --> 03:12.316 align:middle
The weakness abroad will also
weigh on our exports for a time.

03:12.826 --> 03:15.396 align:middle
Moreover, the energy sector
has recently come under stress

03:15.966 --> 03:18.426 align:middle
because of the large drop
in global oil prices.

03:19.266 --> 03:20.576 align:middle
Inflation, which has continued

03:20.636 --> 03:22.976 align:middle
to run below our symmetric
2 percent objective,

03:23.556 --> 03:25.056 align:middle
will likely be held
down this year

03:25.056 --> 03:28.566 align:middle
by the effects of the outbreak.

03:29.616 --> 03:31.966 align:middle
Financial conditions have
also tightened markedly.

03:32.546 --> 03:35.766 align:middle
The cost of credit has risen for
all but the strongest borrowers,

03:36.466 --> 03:38.976 align:middle
and stock markets around
the world are down sharply.

03:40.186 --> 03:42.986 align:middle
Moreover, the rapidly
evolving situation has led

03:42.986 --> 03:46.316 align:middle
to high volatility in financial
markets as everyone tries

03:46.316 --> 03:47.646 align:middle
to assess the path ahead.

03:48.856 --> 03:51.536 align:middle
In the past week, several
important financial markets,

03:51.616 --> 03:54.086 align:middle
including the market for
U.S. Treasury securities,

03:55.006 --> 03:57.936 align:middle
have at times shown signs of
stress and impaired liquidity.

03:59.386 --> 04:01.916 align:middle
The market for Treasury
securities is a critical part

04:01.916 --> 04:04.006 align:middle
of the foundation of the
global financial system.

04:04.906 --> 04:07.746 align:middle
It is generally the most liquid
of all markets and serves

04:07.746 --> 04:11.276 align:middle
as the benchmark by which
many other financial assets

04:11.276 --> 04:11.856 align:middle
are valued.

04:12.696 --> 04:15.646 align:middle
It plays an important role in
allowing households and firms

04:15.646 --> 04:18.886 align:middle
to earn a safe return
and manage their risks.

04:19.226 --> 04:21.036 align:middle
When stresses arise in
the Treasury market,

04:21.256 --> 04:23.786 align:middle
they can reverberate through
the entire financial system

04:23.916 --> 04:24.556 align:middle
and the economy.

04:25.986 --> 04:27.106 align:middle
To prevent this from happening

04:27.106 --> 04:28.676 align:middle
and to support the
smooth functioning

04:28.676 --> 04:30.356 align:middle
of the Treasury market,
we announced today

04:30.796 --> 04:33.016 align:middle
that we will purchase
at least $500 billion

04:33.016 --> 04:35.406 align:middle
of Treasury securities
over the coming months.

04:36.456 --> 04:38.826 align:middle
Similar stresses have
also emerged in the market

04:38.826 --> 04:40.566 align:middle
for agency mortgage-backed
securities,

04:41.076 --> 04:43.056 align:middle
which is closely linked
to the Treasury market

04:43.646 --> 04:45.546 align:middle
and critically supports
the ability of people

04:45.616 --> 04:47.956 align:middle
to get a mortgage to buy a house

04:48.316 --> 04:50.126 align:middle
or refinance an existing
mortgage.

04:51.546 --> 04:53.176 align:middle
To improve the functioning
of this market

04:53.236 --> 04:56.126 align:middle
and to ensure the effective
transmission of monetary policy

04:56.126 --> 04:59.086 align:middle
to borrowers in the economy,
we will also purchase

04:59.166 --> 05:02.996 align:middle
at least $200 billion of agency
mortgage-backed securities

05:02.996 --> 05:05.936 align:middle
over coming months and
immediately cease the runoff

05:05.936 --> 05:07.456 align:middle
of these securities
in our portfolio.

05:08.916 --> 05:11.776 align:middle
While the primary purpose of
these securities purchases is

05:11.776 --> 05:13.786 align:middle
to restore smooth
market functioning

05:13.786 --> 05:15.616 align:middle
so that credit can
continue to flow,

05:16.376 --> 05:18.756 align:middle
the purchases will also
foster more accommodative

05:18.806 --> 05:19.596 align:middle
financial conditions.

05:21.806 --> 05:24.706 align:middle
The Federal Reserve announced
a number of other actions today

05:24.796 --> 05:27.526 align:middle
to support the flow of credit
to households and businesses,

05:27.646 --> 05:29.736 align:middle
thereby promoting our
maximum employment

05:29.736 --> 05:30.856 align:middle
and price stability goals.

05:31.186 --> 05:32.646 align:middle
Of these, I will highlight two.

05:33.406 --> 05:36.496 align:middle
First, we reduced the interest
rate on discount window loans

05:36.536 --> 05:40.426 align:middle
by 1½ percentage points,
bringing that rate to ¼ percent.

05:41.256 --> 05:43.466 align:middle
The discount window
plays an important role

05:43.466 --> 05:45.996 align:middle
in supporting liquidity and
stability in the banking system,

05:46.236 --> 05:48.696 align:middle
and we encourage banks to
turn to the discount window

05:49.216 --> 05:51.906 align:middle
to help meet demands for credit
from households and businesses.

05:52.186 --> 05:55.056 align:middle
To make the discount
window more effective,

05:55.116 --> 05:57.136 align:middle
we will also offer
discount window loans

05:57.136 --> 05:58.796 align:middle
for periods up to 90 days.

06:00.956 --> 06:03.026 align:middle
Because of the importance
of the U.S. dollar

06:03.026 --> 06:05.436 align:middle
in the global economy, strains
in markets for borrowing

06:05.436 --> 06:08.926 align:middle
and lending dollars overseas
can disrupt financial conditions

06:08.926 --> 06:12.226 align:middle
here in the United States.

06:13.016 --> 06:14.556 align:middle
To guard against
such disruptions,

06:14.636 --> 06:16.846 align:middle
the Federal Reserve
maintains swap lines

06:16.846 --> 06:18.366 align:middle
with five major central banks.

06:19.516 --> 06:21.666 align:middle
When dollar funding
pressures emerge abroad,

06:22.026 --> 06:23.966 align:middle
those central banks can
contain the pressures

06:23.966 --> 06:26.106 align:middle
in their jurisdictions
and prevent them

06:26.156 --> 06:28.226 align:middle
from impeding the flow
of credit here at home.

06:29.316 --> 06:30.656 align:middle
To address potential pressures

06:30.656 --> 06:32.426 align:middle
in these markets during
the current period

06:32.426 --> 06:35.956 align:middle
of elevated uncertainty, we
made a coordinated announcement

06:36.006 --> 06:37.816 align:middle
with the Bank of Canada,
the Bank of England,

06:38.026 --> 06:40.516 align:middle
the Bank of Japan, the
European Central Bank,

06:40.836 --> 06:43.576 align:middle
and the Swiss National
Bank to reduce the pricing

06:43.646 --> 06:44.976 align:middle
on our dollar swap lines.

06:45.826 --> 06:49.066 align:middle
In addition, our central bank
counterparts will begin offering

06:49.066 --> 06:52.226 align:middle
dollars to institutions in
their jurisdictions for a term

06:52.226 --> 06:56.346 align:middle
of 84 days in addition to
the usual one-week operation.

06:57.556 --> 07:00.336 align:middle
These long-standing
arrangements carry no risk

07:00.486 --> 07:02.716 align:middle
to the Federal Reserve or
to the American taxpayer.

07:04.816 --> 07:07.486 align:middle
I won't go into detail on the
other actions we took today,

07:07.486 --> 07:10.406 align:middle
but they involved eliminating
reserve requirements for banks

07:10.406 --> 07:13.316 align:middle
and encouraging banks to
make use of intraday credit

07:13.316 --> 07:15.416 align:middle
with the Federal Reserve
and to use their capital

07:15.416 --> 07:18.016 align:middle
and liquidity buffers
as they support lending

07:18.016 --> 07:19.156 align:middle
to households and businesses.

07:19.706 --> 07:23.386 align:middle
The actions we have announced
today will help American

07:23.446 --> 07:25.236 align:middle
families and businesses
and, indeed,

07:25.236 --> 07:27.656 align:middle
our entire economy weather
this difficult period

07:28.116 --> 07:31.006 align:middle
and will foster a more
vigor-more vigorous return

07:31.056 --> 07:34.276 align:middle
to normal once the disruptions
from the coronavirus abate.

07:36.036 --> 07:38.456 align:middle
We will continue to
closely monitor economic

07:38.456 --> 07:40.546 align:middle
and financial developments
and their implications

07:40.546 --> 07:41.596 align:middle
for the economic outlook.

07:42.106 --> 07:44.496 align:middle
We are prepared to use
our full range of tools

07:44.496 --> 07:46.616 align:middle
to support the flow of credit
to households and business,

07:46.616 --> 07:48.856 align:middle
to help keep the economy strong,

07:49.416 --> 07:51.126 align:middle
and to promote our
maximum employment

07:51.126 --> 07:52.356 align:middle
and price stability goals.

07:53.526 --> 07:56.086 align:middle
Finally, let me note that
today's FOMC meeting today was

07:56.086 --> 07:58.686 align:middle
in lieu of the meeting scheduled
for next Tuesday and Wednesday.

07:59.516 --> 08:00.416 align:middle
Thank you.

08:00.416 --> 08:02.806 align:middle
I'll be-I'll be happy to
take a few of your questions.

08:03.826 --> 08:04.226 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

08:04.226 --> 08:05.986 align:middle
The Chair will now
take some questions.

08:05.986 --> 08:09.006 align:middle
Please dial "star
1" on your phone

08:09.006 --> 08:10.996 align:middle
to indicate you want
to ask a question.

08:11.176 --> 08:13.186 align:middle
You will be unmuted
when you are called on.

08:14.196 --> 08:43.356 align:middle
[Extended silence] Okay, CNBC.

08:43.966 --> 08:47.926 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

08:48.316 --> 08:48.816 align:middle
Thank you.

08:49.546 --> 08:54.616 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, how effective do
you expect this-these actions

08:54.616 --> 08:55.016 align:middle
to be?

08:55.016 --> 08:58.316 align:middle
Do you-do you think a recession
is inevitable and/or can

08:58.316 --> 08:58.936 align:middle
be avoided?

08:59.936 --> 09:03.446 align:middle
Secondly, there was talk
about additional programs

09:03.446 --> 09:06.096 align:middle
that could come out-for example,
a Primary Dealer Credit Facility

09:06.516 --> 09:07.786 align:middle
or a Term Auction Facility.

09:07.786 --> 09:09.896 align:middle
Do you expect those
to be announced soon?

09:10.286 --> 09:10.626 align:middle
Thank you.

09:10.626 --> 09:11.136 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:12.116 --> 09:12.676 align:middle
Thank you.

09:12.676 --> 09:17.946 align:middle
So, based on our discussions
today and many conversations

09:17.946 --> 09:21.666 align:middle
with participants, it's fair
to say that we have a range

09:21.666 --> 09:23.306 align:middle
of views about the
path of the economy,

09:23.306 --> 09:25.406 align:middle
as is always the
case with the Fed.

09:26.036 --> 09:29.236 align:middle
But, in general, I'd say
that we see the U.S. economy

09:29.236 --> 09:31.726 align:middle
as having been in quite a strong
position before the arrival

09:31.726 --> 09:32.216 align:middle
of the virus.

09:32.996 --> 09:35.106 align:middle
We see that it is likely
that the measures we take

09:35.106 --> 09:37.886 align:middle
to protect ourselves from the
virus will involve withdrawing

09:37.976 --> 09:40.706 align:middle
from or reducing certain
activities-thinking there

09:40.706 --> 09:45.206 align:middle
of travel, leisure, hospitality,
those things-and that's going

09:45.206 --> 09:47.626 align:middle
to mean lower economic
activity for a period of time.

09:47.676 --> 09:48.986 align:middle
These are choices that we make

09:49.446 --> 09:52.496 align:middle
to protect ourselves-appropriate
choices that we make

09:52.496 --> 09:53.946 align:middle
to protect ourselves
from the virus.

09:54.506 --> 09:56.756 align:middle
So that means that the second
quarter is probably going

09:56.756 --> 09:59.786 align:middle
to be weak-in fact,
in the view of many,

10:01.046 --> 10:04.646 align:middle
output declining-output
lower in the second quarter

10:04.646 --> 10:05.646 align:middle
than it was in the
first quarter.

10:06.136 --> 10:09.906 align:middle
After that, it's very hard to
say how big the effects will be

10:09.906 --> 10:11.336 align:middle
or how long they will last.

10:11.486 --> 10:13.636 align:middle
And that's going to
depend, of course,

10:14.046 --> 10:16.396 align:middle
on how widely the virus
spreads, which is something

10:16.396 --> 10:19.956 align:middle
that is highly uncertain, and I
would say, in fact, unknowable.

10:21.386 --> 10:23.306 align:middle
We do know that the
virus will run its course

10:23.366 --> 10:25.496 align:middle
and that the U.S. economy
will resume a normal level

10:25.496 --> 10:26.096 align:middle
of activity.

10:26.096 --> 10:28.976 align:middle
In the meantime, the Fed will
continue to use our tools

10:28.976 --> 10:31.166 align:middle
to support the flow of credit
to households and businesses

10:31.636 --> 10:33.826 align:middle
and support demand with
monetary policy-ultimately,

10:33.826 --> 10:36.396 align:middle
to do what we can to
see that the recovery is

10:36.396 --> 10:37.726 align:middle
as vigorous as possible.

10:38.566 --> 10:41.106 align:middle
You also asked about
other facilities.

10:41.106 --> 10:43.376 align:middle
I would point out that
we have, of course,

10:43.376 --> 10:46.456 align:middle
responded very strongly not
just with interest rates,

10:46.456 --> 10:49.906 align:middle
but also with liquidity
measures today.

10:49.906 --> 10:52.216 align:middle
And we believe that what we
did today will be beneficial

10:52.216 --> 10:53.936 align:middle
to financial markets generally.

10:54.706 --> 10:56.336 align:middle
And as I said in my
statement, we're prepared

10:56.336 --> 10:59.316 align:middle
to use our full range of tools
to support the flow of credit

10:59.316 --> 11:00.466 align:middle
to households and businesses.

11:00.696 --> 11:02.806 align:middle
Thanks. MICHELLE SMITH.

11:02.806 --> 11:03.376 align:middle
Okay, thanks.

11:03.376 --> 11:04.916 align:middle
We'll go to Marketplace radio.

11:04.916 --> 11:06.066 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

11:06.956 --> 11:10.516 align:middle
Hi, Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

11:10.516 --> 11:11.996 align:middle
Chair Powell, I'm wondering,

11:12.176 --> 11:16.366 align:middle
are you considering negative
interest rates at this point?

11:16.366 --> 11:20.106 align:middle
Is there a scenario in which you
think that would be appropriate?

11:20.546 --> 11:21.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:21.576 --> 11:26.436 align:middle
So, as I've noted on a
number of occasions, really,

11:26.526 --> 11:33.116 align:middle
the Committee-as you know,
we did a year-plus-long study

11:33.116 --> 11:36.476 align:middle
of our tools and strategies
and communications.

11:37.196 --> 11:40.316 align:middle
And we, really, at
the end of that,

11:40.316 --> 11:43.036 align:middle
and also when we started
out, we view forward guidance

11:43.036 --> 11:45.346 align:middle
and asset purchases-asset
purchases

11:45.786 --> 11:48.746 align:middle
and also different variations
and combinations of those tools

11:49.236 --> 11:50.676 align:middle
as the basic elements

11:50.676 --> 11:54.446 align:middle
of our toolkit once the federal
funds rate reaches the effective

11:54.446 --> 11:57.996 align:middle
lower bound-so, really, forward
guidance, asset purchases,

11:57.996 --> 11:59.176 align:middle
and combinations of those.

11:59.956 --> 12:02.876 align:middle
You know, we looked at negative
policy rates during the Global

12:02.876 --> 12:05.146 align:middle
Financial Crisis, we
monitored their use

12:05.146 --> 12:07.256 align:middle
in other jurisdictions,
we continue to do so,

12:07.696 --> 12:11.316 align:middle
but we do not see negative
policy rates as likely

12:11.316 --> 12:13.136 align:middle
to be an appropriate
policy response here

12:13.136 --> 12:13.896 align:middle
in the United States.

12:14.076 --> 12:14.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

12:16.126 --> 12:17.836 align:middle
Okay, we're going to
the Wall Street Journal.

12:20.386 --> 12:21.606 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

12:21.696 --> 12:22.926 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

12:22.926 --> 12:24.756 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

12:25.236 --> 12:28.486 align:middle
I guess I wanted to
understand, specifically,

12:28.886 --> 12:33.436 align:middle
how the actions you've announced
this evening will get credit

12:33.436 --> 12:36.846 align:middle
to households and firms that
are going to see large drops

12:36.846 --> 12:38.016 align:middle
in income and revenue.

12:38.666 --> 12:42.766 align:middle
And also, to the extent
that they are needed,

12:42.766 --> 12:45.556 align:middle
what steps would you recommend
the fiscal authorities

12:45.556 --> 12:51.086 align:middle
undertake-again, to make sure
that firms with large drops

12:51.306 --> 12:55.196 align:middle
in income and revenue
don't go bust

12:55.196 --> 12:56.786 align:middle
over the next few
weeks or months?

12:57.456 --> 12:57.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:58.176 --> 12:59.796 align:middle
So let me tell you how,

13:00.366 --> 13:02.356 align:middle
I think-what our
tools can accomplish,

13:02.356 --> 13:04.916 align:middle
and-and really what they're
not designed to accomplish.

13:04.916 --> 13:08.156 align:middle
So what our liquidity
operations-and I'll talk

13:08.156 --> 13:11.616 align:middle
specifically about the Treasury
and MBS purchases-are designed

13:11.616 --> 13:14.346 align:middle
to do is to assure
the proper functioning

13:14.346 --> 13:15.976 align:middle
of the Treasury and MBS markets.

13:16.366 --> 13:19.056 align:middle
The Treasury market is
one of the most important,

13:19.086 --> 13:21.716 align:middle
perhaps the most important,
market in the world.

13:22.156 --> 13:25.276 align:middle
It's probably the most liquid
large market in the world.

13:25.636 --> 13:29.156 align:middle
The MBS market is very
important: It's closely linked

13:29.156 --> 13:31.946 align:middle
to the Treasury market,
and it's the way

13:31.946 --> 13:34.556 align:middle
that our monetary
policy decisions flow

13:34.646 --> 13:36.606 align:middle
through to borrowers
in the real economy.

13:36.606 --> 13:38.746 align:middle
So, two very important markets.

13:38.816 --> 13:42.206 align:middle
And they had reached levels
of very high illiquidity,

13:42.716 --> 13:45.236 align:middle
and-to the point
where we-as you know,

13:45.236 --> 13:48.946 align:middle
last week we offered
financing-very large amounts

13:48.946 --> 13:49.466 align:middle
of financing.

13:49.466 --> 13:52.246 align:middle
And after that, we bought
assets across the curve to try

13:52.246 --> 13:54.586 align:middle
to support liquidity and
return to normal function.

13:55.196 --> 13:57.676 align:middle
And we found that that
helped, but, nonetheless,

13:57.676 --> 13:59.136 align:middle
we thought we had to do more.

13:59.136 --> 14:00.556 align:middle
And we knew what we had to do,

14:00.556 --> 14:03.246 align:middle
which was the asset purchase
programs that we did today.

14:03.816 --> 14:04.576 align:middle
And those are designed

14:04.576 --> 14:07.986 align:middle
to restore those key
markets to normal function.

14:07.986 --> 14:10.756 align:middle
Now, why is that
important to-to everyone?

14:11.156 --> 14:13.606 align:middle
These markets are
part of the foundation

14:13.606 --> 14:14.966 align:middle
of the global financial system

14:14.966 --> 14:17.386 align:middle
and of the United
States financial system.

14:17.616 --> 14:21.136 align:middle
And if they are not functioning
well, then other markets-then

14:21.136 --> 14:22.626 align:middle
that will spread
to other markets.

14:23.096 --> 14:26.696 align:middle
And those are the markets
that-that amount to-that-where

14:26.996 --> 14:29.466 align:middle
households and businesses
typically get credit.

14:29.466 --> 14:32.996 align:middle
So we know that dysfunctional
financial markets-a

14:32.996 --> 14:35.296 align:middle
dysfunctional financial
system, really,

14:35.726 --> 14:37.956 align:middle
can have very big negative
effects on the economy.

14:37.956 --> 14:39.586 align:middle
We saw that during
the financial crisis.

14:39.616 --> 14:41.686 align:middle
This is a very different
situation, but, nonetheless,

14:41.686 --> 14:43.306 align:middle
we thought it was
quite important for us

14:43.366 --> 14:45.566 align:middle
to take strong measures

14:45.596 --> 14:49.436 align:middle
to support-to support
proper market function

14:49.436 --> 14:50.446 align:middle
in these important markets.

14:51.146 --> 14:52.616 align:middle
Now, you asked about households.

14:53.036 --> 14:56.536 align:middle
It's true, we don't have the
tools to reach individuals

14:56.536 --> 14:59.956 align:middle
and particularly small
businesses and other businesses

14:59.956 --> 15:01.286 align:middle
and people who may
be out of work

15:01.286 --> 15:04.066 align:middle
or whose businesses may
not-may experience a period

15:04.066 --> 15:04.896 align:middle
of very low activity.

15:05.176 --> 15:06.446 align:middle
We don't have those tools.

15:06.446 --> 15:08.216 align:middle
We have the tools that
we have, and we use them.

15:08.216 --> 15:09.746 align:middle
I think we've used
them quite aggressively

15:09.746 --> 15:11.196 align:middle
for the benefit of the public.

15:11.296 --> 15:15.876 align:middle
But this is a multifaceted
problem, and it requires answers

15:15.876 --> 15:19.056 align:middle
from different parts of
the government and society.

15:19.056 --> 15:20.666 align:middle
I mean, I think that, by far,

15:20.666 --> 15:22.216 align:middle
the most important
is the response

15:22.276 --> 15:26.086 align:middle
of the health-care workers
and health-care policymakers.

15:26.456 --> 15:28.226 align:middle
After that, fiscal policy.

15:28.226 --> 15:30.956 align:middle
We've had one round of fiscal
policy, another coming.

15:31.046 --> 15:35.516 align:middle
I think fiscal policy is a
way to direct relief, really,

15:35.556 --> 15:38.336 align:middle
to particular populations
and groups.

15:38.826 --> 15:41.346 align:middle
Monetary policy has a
role, and it really is in,

15:41.806 --> 15:46.036 align:middle
you know-our original role
was providing liquidity

15:46.036 --> 15:48.786 align:middle
to financial systems when
they're under stress,

15:48.846 --> 15:50.816 align:middle
and that's-that's really
part of what we did today.

15:51.106 --> 15:52.526 align:middle
The other role is
to support demand

15:52.526 --> 15:54.486 align:middle
through lower interest
rates, and we did that.

15:55.056 --> 15:56.646 align:middle
You-you asked about
fiscal as well,

15:56.646 --> 15:58.346 align:middle
and I guess I've
sort of covered that.

15:58.976 --> 16:02.266 align:middle
We do think fiscal response
is-is critical, and we're happy

16:02.266 --> 16:04.906 align:middle
to see that those measures
are being considered,

16:04.906 --> 16:07.946 align:middle
and we-we hope they
are effective.

16:08.646 --> 16:09.366 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:10.086 --> 16:12.156 align:middle
Okay, we're going to
go to Fox Business.

16:12.156 --> 16:13.716 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

16:15.056 --> 16:16.346 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

16:16.346 --> 16:18.516 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business Network.

16:18.516 --> 16:22.096 align:middle
In your statement, you say
that the market measures

16:22.096 --> 16:23.356 align:middle
of inflation have declined.

16:23.356 --> 16:27.086 align:middle
The Fed has undershot
inflation in the last year,

16:27.396 --> 16:30.006 align:middle
and that's become one of the
focuses of the FOMC meetings.

16:30.006 --> 16:32.796 align:middle
Now, does the Federal
Reserve see deflation coming,

16:32.796 --> 16:35.386 align:middle
and is this large cut
meant to get in front

16:35.386 --> 16:37.096 align:middle
of any more inflation?

16:37.096 --> 16:38.746 align:middle
Plus, as a second question,

16:38.746 --> 16:41.666 align:middle
would the Fed consider buying
securities outside of Treasuries

16:41.666 --> 16:42.926 align:middle
and mortgage-backed securities?

16:43.416 --> 16:43.706 align:middle
Thank you.

16:44.436 --> 16:44.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:44.896 --> 16:48.566 align:middle
Right. So, on-on
inflation, you know,

16:48.566 --> 16:50.406 align:middle
the-the economy's
performed so well, really,

16:50.536 --> 16:53.756 align:middle
over the last few years, and
right up through January:

16:54.796 --> 16:57.206 align:middle
historically low unemployment-50
year-low unemployment

16:57.206 --> 16:59.976 align:middle
for a couple of years,
11 years-almost 11 years

16:59.976 --> 17:02.216 align:middle
of expansion so far, and

17:02.216 --> 17:04.256 align:middle
yet inflation lingered
below 2 percent.

17:04.256 --> 17:07.426 align:middle
So we've been concerned that
inflation not move down further.

17:07.656 --> 17:09.386 align:middle
It's not at all a
question of deflation.

17:09.386 --> 17:12.716 align:middle
It was a question of not getting
inflation up to our target

17:12.716 --> 17:14.686 align:middle
on a robust, symmetric
kind of a way.

17:15.336 --> 17:18.236 align:middle
So, with this coronavirus
arriving,

17:18.386 --> 17:20.686 align:middle
we judged that the-the
net effects

17:20.766 --> 17:24.566 align:middle
of this will be to-to
have inflation move

17:24.566 --> 17:25.726 align:middle
down even a little bit more.

17:26.176 --> 17:28.626 align:middle
And it's-it's not a
question-it's not-it's not an

17:28.626 --> 17:29.486 align:middle
issue of deflation.

17:29.486 --> 17:31.196 align:middle
Again, it's just an issue

17:31.196 --> 17:33.786 align:middle
of inflation probably
falling even a bit further.

17:33.786 --> 17:36.486 align:middle
We expect, with uncertainty,
that,

17:36.486 --> 17:39.876 align:middle
you know-that it will fall a
little bit further, at least,

17:39.916 --> 17:41.986 align:middle
below where it has been.

17:41.986 --> 17:44.316 align:middle
We are-of course, we don't
have the legal authority

17:44.316 --> 17:48.436 align:middle
to buy other securities other
than the ones we already buy,

17:48.436 --> 17:50.116 align:middle
and we're not seeking
authority to do so.

17:50.116 --> 17:52.576 align:middle
We haven't discussed
that at the-at the FOMC,

17:52.576 --> 17:57.616 align:middle
and it's not-it's not legal
authority that we're seeking.

17:57.616 --> 17:58.196 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:58.286 --> 18:01.896 align:middle
Okay, we'll go to
the Financial Times.

18:03.266 --> 18:03.796 align:middle
BRENDAN GREELEY.

18:04.196 --> 18:05.176 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

18:05.176 --> 18:07.906 align:middle
This is Brendan Greeley
with the Financial Times.

18:08.546 --> 18:12.016 align:middle
You have talked about the
mortgage-backed security

18:12.016 --> 18:13.436 align:middle
purchases and the
Treasury purchases

18:13.436 --> 18:17.806 align:middle
as a stability-a
stability operation.

18:17.806 --> 18:19.726 align:middle
I'm pretty sure that
every person

18:19.726 --> 18:22.646 align:middle
on this call has an editor
asking to determine whether

18:22.646 --> 18:24.036 align:middle
or not this is quantitative
easing.

18:24.196 --> 18:26.846 align:middle
How do we draw a distinction
between these asset purchases

18:27.046 --> 18:28.726 align:middle
and something you
may do in the future

18:28.726 --> 18:30.696 align:middle
that is explicit
quantitative easing?

18:30.696 --> 18:33.996 align:middle
Or can we think of
this as the first step

18:33.996 --> 18:34.916 align:middle
in quantitative easing?

18:35.136 --> 18:35.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:35.906 --> 18:38.836 align:middle
So what-what I can tell you
definitively is what the purpose

18:38.836 --> 18:40.416 align:middle
of the asset purchases was,

18:40.416 --> 18:41.926 align:middle
and I mentioned this
in my statement.

18:41.926 --> 18:45.706 align:middle
And it really is to support
the availability of credit

18:45.976 --> 18:47.276 align:middle
in the economy-households

18:47.276 --> 18:49.566 align:middle
and businesses-and thereby
support the overall economy.

18:49.616 --> 18:50.426 align:middle
How do they do that?

18:50.826 --> 18:53.156 align:middle
They do that by supporting
proper market functioning

18:53.156 --> 18:54.886 align:middle
in the Treasury market
and the MBS market.

18:55.346 --> 18:59.016 align:middle
So you see-and I think you'll
see-as these purchases roll

18:59.016 --> 19:00.686 align:middle
forward, you will see
the Treasury market

19:00.686 --> 19:04.876 align:middle
and the MBS market returning
to normal market function,

19:05.516 --> 19:07.776 align:middle
and that will actually
support economic activity.

19:07.776 --> 19:09.686 align:middle
That will be a positive
for economic activity.

19:09.686 --> 19:13.366 align:middle
In terms of what it's labeled,
that's of less interest to me.

19:13.366 --> 19:15.666 align:middle
I really think we are very clear

19:15.666 --> 19:17.506 align:middle
in our minds what
we're doing this for

19:17.956 --> 19:20.116 align:middle
and what the logical
effects would be,

19:20.116 --> 19:23.306 align:middle
which would be-which would be
both better market function

19:23.306 --> 19:26.626 align:middle
and-and-you know, when
there's better market function,

19:26.626 --> 19:31.206 align:middle
it will be-you know, policy
will be supporting the

19:31.206 --> 19:31.736 align:middle
economy better.

19:32.686 --> 19:33.156 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:33.926 --> 19:35.866 align:middle
Okay, Bloomberg TV.

19:35.866 --> 19:36.706 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

19:37.716 --> 19:39.026 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

19:39.296 --> 19:42.246 align:middle
I want to ask some
timing questions here.

19:42.246 --> 19:46.536 align:middle
First, on the-whatever
it is, QE or not QE,

19:47.116 --> 19:48.596 align:middle
you talk in the press release

19:48.596 --> 19:50.236 align:middle
about increase "over
coming months."

19:50.606 --> 19:52.226 align:middle
Is there a timetable for that?

19:52.226 --> 19:55.276 align:middle
How long are we talking
about-$700 billion, obviously,

19:55.576 --> 19:59.676 align:middle
over two months is a lot
different from $700 billion

19:59.676 --> 20:02.476 align:middle
over the course of a year.

20:02.476 --> 20:05.136 align:middle
And do you have a date
where you would reevaluate

20:05.136 --> 20:09.256 align:middle
and perhaps add more
or end the program?

20:09.546 --> 20:12.196 align:middle
And the same with
the interest rate cut

20:12.196 --> 20:15.736 align:middle
to zero-how long do you
think that might last,

20:16.486 --> 20:20.186 align:middle
and would you be inclined
to quickly go back

20:20.536 --> 20:23.796 align:middle
to a higher level,
the 100 basis points

20:23.796 --> 20:25.196 align:middle
or more that you cut it from?

20:25.576 --> 20:28.396 align:middle
Or would you anticipate
that this will be a long,

20:28.666 --> 20:30.476 align:middle
slow process going back?

20:31.446 --> 20:31.736 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:33.056 --> 20:34.076 align:middle
Okay, so-sorry.

20:34.076 --> 20:35.096 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:35.626 --> 20:38.036 align:middle
Mike, can you ask your question?

20:38.186 --> 20:38.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:38.626 --> 20:39.316 align:middle
What was it again?

20:39.316 --> 20:40.026 align:middle
What was the question again?

20:40.026 --> 20:40.446 align:middle
Just tell me.

20:40.446 --> 20:41.336 align:middle
I can't read my writing.

20:41.336 --> 20:41.746 align:middle
[Laughter] MICHAEL MCKEE.

20:41.746 --> 20:44.076 align:middle
I was asking about timing.

20:44.076 --> 20:45.686 align:middle
How long a period- CHAIR POWELL.

20:45.686 --> 20:46.206 align:middle
Ah, okay, great.

20:46.206 --> 20:46.526 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

20:46.526 --> 20:49.236 align:middle
-are we talking about
for the QE purchases,

20:49.236 --> 20:52.076 align:middle
and how long do you
anticipate keeping rates low,

20:52.076 --> 20:54.246 align:middle
and how fast would you
bring them back up again?

20:54.566 --> 20:54.796 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:54.796 --> 20:55.306 align:middle
Okay, sorry.

20:55.926 --> 20:58.666 align:middle
So that's the "in
coming months" language.

20:58.996 --> 21:00.436 align:middle
Let me tell you what
we were doing there.

21:00.436 --> 21:02.526 align:middle
So there's no monthly cap here.

21:02.526 --> 21:03.836 align:middle
There's no weekly cap.

21:04.256 --> 21:07.886 align:middle
The Desk is going to go
out, and it's going to buy

21:07.886 --> 21:13.416 align:middle
at a strong rate that is-that
we think will restore market

21:13.846 --> 21:18.726 align:middle
function-restore liquidity as
quickly as it can be restored.

21:19.276 --> 21:23.306 align:middle
And that language is open ended,
and it's meant to send a signal

21:23.306 --> 21:27.066 align:middle
to the market that-that,
you know, we're not going

21:27.066 --> 21:29.396 align:middle
to be bound by, for
example, $60 billion a month

21:29.396 --> 21:30.146 align:middle
or anything like that.

21:30.146 --> 21:33.126 align:middle
We're going to go in
strong starting tomorrow,

21:33.576 --> 21:35.676 align:middle
and we're going to buy across
the curve, and we're going

21:35.676 --> 21:38.146 align:middle
to buy MBS, and we're really
going to use our tools

21:38.666 --> 21:39.706 align:middle
to do what we need to do here,

21:39.706 --> 21:43.906 align:middle
which is restore these important
markets to normal function.

21:43.906 --> 21:45.286 align:middle
So that-that's really
what that is.

21:45.456 --> 21:49.736 align:middle
As far as-as the policy
rate, what we've said is

21:49.736 --> 21:52.166 align:middle
that we will maintain
the rate at this level

21:52.166 --> 21:55.476 align:middle
until we're confident that the
economy has weathered recent

21:55.476 --> 21:59.196 align:middle
events and is on track to
achieve our maximum employment

21:59.196 --> 22:00.366 align:middle
and price stability goals.

22:01.256 --> 22:03.466 align:middle
So, you can see, that's the
test we've written down.

22:03.516 --> 22:07.266 align:middle
And if you-if you look at it,
you'll see that some things have

22:07.266 --> 22:09.736 align:middle
to happen before we'd
consider-we'd have

22:09.736 --> 22:14.306 align:middle
to become confident of-that
the economy has weathered these

22:14.306 --> 22:16.346 align:middle
recent events and
also is on track

22:16.396 --> 22:17.776 align:middle
to achieve our maximum
employment

22:17.776 --> 22:18.726 align:middle
and price stability goals.

22:18.726 --> 22:21.036 align:middle
So that's a test
we'll be looking at,

22:21.256 --> 22:22.956 align:middle
and I think it suggests
that, you know,

22:22.956 --> 22:25.656 align:middle
we're going to be
watching and willing

22:25.926 --> 22:27.186 align:middle
to be patient, certainly.

22:27.706 --> 22:28.186 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:28.646 --> 22:30.536 align:middle
Okay, we're going to go
to the Associated Press.

22:32.166 --> 22:33.326 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

22:33.326 --> 22:35.696 align:middle
Hi, it's Chris Rugaber at AP.

22:35.896 --> 22:39.166 align:middle
In 2008, you had a
lot of coordination

22:39.166 --> 22:42.616 align:middle
between the White House
at that time, the Fed,

22:42.616 --> 22:45.506 align:middle
the Treasury-Bernanke
doing a lot of things

22:45.506 --> 22:46.876 align:middle
with Paulson and so forth.

22:47.456 --> 22:49.436 align:middle
You know, today the White
House had its press conference

22:49.436 --> 22:50.056 align:middle
at 5:00.

22:50.086 --> 22:51.726 align:middle
You guys are doing
something a little separate.

22:52.376 --> 22:55.226 align:middle
Is there-do you think
it would be more helpful

22:55.226 --> 22:58.766 align:middle
to markets-perhaps, in
the broader economy,

22:58.766 --> 23:00.756 align:middle
to have a more coordinated
response?

23:00.756 --> 23:03.326 align:middle
Is that something we might
look for in the future?

23:03.796 --> 23:05.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:05.366 --> 23:06.876 align:middle
Well, I would say this.

23:06.936 --> 23:11.406 align:middle
You know, we have
different tools.

23:11.406 --> 23:14.196 align:middle
I think we do, actually,
work pretty closely

23:14.196 --> 23:16.406 align:middle
with the Treasury Department
and-and cooperate with them

23:16.406 --> 23:19.216 align:middle
on things, with clear
lines of delineation.

23:19.216 --> 23:22.316 align:middle
For example, the Treasury
Department has authority

23:22.316 --> 23:23.256 align:middle
over fiscal policy.

23:23.256 --> 23:24.316 align:middle
That's not our job.

23:24.746 --> 23:26.966 align:middle
It's their job, with other
parts of the Administration.

23:27.736 --> 23:30.266 align:middle
It's just that-like that
for us with monetary policy.

23:30.266 --> 23:33.306 align:middle
We have sole responsibility
for monetary policy

23:33.306 --> 23:35.556 align:middle
and strong instructions
from Congress to conduct it

23:35.556 --> 23:37.056 align:middle
in an independent,
nonpolitical way.

23:37.646 --> 23:41.026 align:middle
So those two things
are-one for each of us.

23:41.536 --> 23:43.286 align:middle
But in the middle
there, there are areas

23:43.286 --> 23:45.836 align:middle
where we-where we can cooperate.

23:46.536 --> 23:48.316 align:middle
And also, there's
a long history.

23:48.316 --> 23:50.096 align:middle
You would want the
finance ministry

23:50.096 --> 23:51.426 align:middle
and the treasury [and the
central bank] to be talking

23:51.426 --> 23:52.876 align:middle
in every major economy
in the world.

23:52.876 --> 23:53.366 align:middle
You would want them

23:53.366 --> 23:56.116 align:middle
to be regularly exchanging
information-professional

23:56.116 --> 23:58.956 align:middle
relationships not
just at the, you know,

23:59.456 --> 24:01.936 align:middle
the Chair-to-Treasury-Secretary
level,

24:01.936 --> 24:03.276 align:middle
but also through
the organization.

24:03.586 --> 24:07.366 align:middle
For example, the New York Fed
is the Treasury's agent-fiscal

24:07.366 --> 24:09.816 align:middle
agent, as you know, so
there's a lot that goes

24:09.816 --> 24:12.306 align:middle
into debt management and
the issuance of debt,

24:12.306 --> 24:13.516 align:middle
which happens at
the New York Fed.

24:13.516 --> 24:17.556 align:middle
So I think we do,
actually-we have professional,

24:17.556 --> 24:21.106 align:middle
good working relationships up
and down the chain there, and,

24:21.316 --> 24:25.076 align:middle
you know, that-that seems
to me to be working.

24:25.456 --> 24:29.126 align:middle
And there is-there's also
quite a lot of coordination

24:29.126 --> 24:30.766 align:middle
in other respects
with-of course,

24:30.766 --> 24:32.586 align:middle
with foreign central banks,
but you didn't ask about that.

24:33.636 --> 24:34.346 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:34.346 --> 24:44.156 align:middle
Okay, we're going to go
to the Washington Post.

24:45.426 --> 24:46.606 align:middle
[Silence] Washington
Post, are you there?

24:47.226 --> 24:48.206 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

24:49.496 --> 24:51.186 align:middle
Hey, sorry, I'm here.

24:51.256 --> 24:53.296 align:middle
I think-hi, Chair Powell.

24:53.636 --> 24:55.006 align:middle
Two questions this evening.

24:55.006 --> 24:58.416 align:middle
First, have you personally
been tested for coronavirus,

24:58.596 --> 25:00.346 align:middle
and are you working from home?

25:00.346 --> 25:03.206 align:middle
If you could shed some light on
the precautions you're taking.

25:03.516 --> 25:07.406 align:middle
And, second, do you believe
the Fed has sufficient tools

25:07.406 --> 25:12.216 align:middle
at the moment, or are you
seeking congressional action

25:12.216 --> 25:14.106 align:middle
to expand your tool set?

25:15.116 --> 25:16.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:16.386 --> 25:17.746 align:middle
So I feel fine.

25:17.796 --> 25:22.666 align:middle
I feel very well, and so there's
no reason for me to be tested.

25:23.246 --> 25:26.696 align:middle
We are observing-we're trying
to model good behavior here,

25:26.696 --> 25:29.526 align:middle
so we're observing quite
a lot of teleworking.

25:29.856 --> 25:32.216 align:middle
And also, we're-you know,
we're not having a lot of big,

25:32.216 --> 25:33.646 align:middle
crowded meetings-we're
not having any big,

25:33.646 --> 25:34.066 align:middle
crowded meetings.

25:34.066 --> 25:36.246 align:middle
So we're trying to
observe all of the things

25:36.296 --> 25:38.766 align:middle
that the professionals
tell us to do.

25:39.126 --> 25:42.286 align:middle
I do expect to do some
teleworking because I want

25:42.286 --> 25:44.586 align:middle
to model that-that it's okay
for the people to do so.

25:44.586 --> 25:46.096 align:middle
In fact, it's important
for them to do so.

25:46.096 --> 25:47.036 align:middle
So I will do that.

25:47.906 --> 25:51.146 align:middle
In terms of our-of our toolkit,
let me say first that, you know,

25:51.146 --> 25:54.986 align:middle
I think we do have plenty of
space to adjust our policy.

25:55.476 --> 25:59.606 align:middle
I mentioned our liquidity
tools-you know, we have a lot

25:59.666 --> 26:02.156 align:middle
of power in our liquidity
tools, and, as I mentioned,

26:02.156 --> 26:03.366 align:middle
we're prepared to use them.

26:03.846 --> 26:05.556 align:middle
In addition, we've got
plenty of space left

26:05.556 --> 26:08.486 align:middle
to offer forward
guidance, asset purchases,

26:08.486 --> 26:11.556 align:middle
and adjust those policies,
so I think we do have room.

26:12.126 --> 26:16.646 align:middle
We-we have not at
all made a decision

26:16.646 --> 26:20.476 align:middle
to request further-further tools

26:20.476 --> 26:22.196 align:middle
or authorities from
Congress, no.

26:22.356 --> 26:23.946 align:middle
We-we have not made
that decision.

26:24.076 --> 26:26.386 align:middle
It is not something we're
actively considering right now.

26:27.186 --> 26:28.266 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:28.696 --> 26:30.096 align:middle
Okay, we'll go to
the New York Times.

26:30.686 --> 26:32.986 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

26:33.576 --> 26:34.316 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

26:34.316 --> 26:35.756 align:middle
This is Jeanna Smialek
from the New York Times.

26:35.756 --> 26:37.396 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

26:37.796 --> 26:41.586 align:middle
I'm just curious-you know,
obviously, you held this meeting

26:41.586 --> 26:44.016 align:middle
in lieu of your meeting
later this week.

26:44.326 --> 26:47.486 align:middle
Will you still release a
Summary of Economic Projections?

26:47.486 --> 26:52.216 align:middle
And if not, how should we
understand how you guys are

26:52.216 --> 27:05.276 align:middle
thinking about the economy
as the coronavirus shapes up?

27:06.496 --> 27:07.346 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:07.456 --> 27:12.206 align:middle
So why no-I guess you're
asking, why no SEP,

27:12.206 --> 27:13.136 align:middle
and what's our forecast?

27:13.136 --> 27:15.466 align:middle
So a couple reasons
we didn't do an SEP.

27:15.606 --> 27:17.966 align:middle
First, we decided on
Thursday to move the meeting

27:17.966 --> 27:20.766 align:middle
up by three days to today,

27:20.766 --> 27:23.166 align:middle
and that's before the
SEPs are generally filed.

27:23.166 --> 27:24.566 align:middle
They hadn't been filled
out, and we-frankly,

27:24.566 --> 27:26.706 align:middle
we spent our time
focused on getting ready

27:26.706 --> 27:27.836 align:middle
to make these announcements.

27:27.996 --> 27:29.596 align:middle
Second, and, you know, a number

27:29.876 --> 27:33.416 align:middle
of FOMC participants had already
reached out to make the point

27:33.416 --> 27:38.256 align:middle
that the economic outlook is
evolving on a daily basis,

27:38.326 --> 27:41.056 align:middle
and it really is depending
heavily on the spread

27:41.136 --> 27:45.436 align:middle
of the virus and the
measures taken to affect it

27:45.666 --> 27:46.826 align:middle
and how long that goes on.

27:46.826 --> 27:48.566 align:middle
And that is just not
something that's knowable.

27:48.566 --> 27:51.906 align:middle
So, actually, writing
down a forecast

27:51.906 --> 27:55.356 align:middle
in that circumstance didn't
seem to be useful, and, in fact,

27:55.476 --> 27:57.136 align:middle
it could have been
more of an obstacle

27:57.266 --> 27:58.786 align:middle
to clear communication
than a help.

27:59.216 --> 28:00.506 align:middle
I do expect that we'll return

28:00.626 --> 28:03.536 align:middle
to the quarterly-regular
quarterly cycle

28:03.536 --> 28:04.246 align:middle
in June, however.

28:04.836 --> 28:11.906 align:middle
In terms of the forecast,
so, as I mentioned, you know,

28:11.906 --> 28:13.296 align:middle
we see the U.S. economy starting

28:13.296 --> 28:17.576 align:middle
out strong-from a strong
position, different-different

28:17.576 --> 28:19.276 align:middle
as we-as we talked
through it today.

28:19.276 --> 28:20.636 align:middle
People have a range
of perspectives

28:20.636 --> 28:22.806 align:middle
on the way the year
is going to go,

28:22.936 --> 28:25.006 align:middle
but I think there's
a pretty broad sense

28:25.106 --> 28:27.356 align:middle
that the second quarter
will be a weak quarter,

28:27.356 --> 28:29.886 align:middle
with probably out-you know,
output declining a bit.

28:30.356 --> 28:33.376 align:middle
After that, it becomes hard to
say, you know, what'll happen

28:33.376 --> 28:35.756 align:middle
in the third and fourth quarters
and thereafter, and it's going

28:35.756 --> 28:37.956 align:middle
to depend, again, on-on
the path of the virus.

28:38.606 --> 28:42.766 align:middle
So the thing we do know is that
we'll be here doing our job

28:42.936 --> 28:46.986 align:middle
and supporting the flow
of credit and there

28:46.986 --> 28:49.136 align:middle
to do what we can when the
recovery comes to make it

28:49.136 --> 28:49.976 align:middle
as vigorous as possible.

28:50.806 --> 28:52.116 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

28:52.116 --> 28:54.056 align:middle
Okay. Now we'll go
to Bloomberg News.

28:54.616 --> 28:55.646 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

28:55.776 --> 28:56.886 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

28:56.886 --> 28:58.516 align:middle
Craig Torres at Bloomberg News.

28:59.196 --> 29:03.166 align:middle
You say the path of the
economy more or less depends

29:03.166 --> 29:04.706 align:middle
on the path of the virus.

29:05.356 --> 29:08.566 align:middle
How important is
fiscal policy now

29:08.566 --> 29:13.816 align:middle
in determining whether this
sudden stop in activity is long

29:13.816 --> 29:16.396 align:middle
or short and of high
human consequence

29:16.396 --> 29:18.006 align:middle
or little human consequence?

29:18.696 --> 29:21.736 align:middle
And, second, can you talk
a little bit about what's

29:21.736 --> 29:26.016 align:middle
on your dashboard for economic
and financial indicators

29:26.016 --> 29:28.906 align:middle
that you're going to
be watching day to day?

29:28.906 --> 29:31.766 align:middle
I know Vice Chair Clarida said

29:32.176 --> 29:35.456 align:middle
that we should start seeing
the data show up in April.

29:35.576 --> 29:36.736 align:middle
What are you going
to be watching,

29:36.736 --> 29:37.916 align:middle
and what should we be watching?

29:38.196 --> 29:38.756 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:39.086 --> 29:40.786 align:middle
So, in terms of fiscal policy,

29:40.786 --> 29:42.906 align:middle
I think in the first
instance-and you're-you're

29:42.906 --> 29:46.436 align:middle
seeing some of this now-is the
thing that fiscal policy and,

29:46.436 --> 29:49.566 align:middle
really, only fiscal policy
can do is reach out directly

29:49.596 --> 29:51.906 align:middle
to affected industries,
affected workers,

29:52.466 --> 29:53.726 align:middle
and we've seen some of that.

29:53.726 --> 29:56.976 align:middle
So, that's-that's an important
job for fiscal policy.

29:57.716 --> 30:00.056 align:middle
More broadly, after that,

30:00.056 --> 30:03.706 align:middle
there's the question of-of
fiscal-broader fiscal stimulus,

30:03.706 --> 30:05.886 align:middle
and I think that'll depend
on the path of the economy.

30:05.936 --> 30:09.476 align:middle
I-I think there is such a wide
range of possibilities here

30:10.076 --> 30:12.166 align:middle
that it's some-it's
very hard to predict.

30:12.166 --> 30:14.736 align:middle
But there could certainly
be-there could certainly be,

30:14.736 --> 30:17.596 align:middle
you know, a need for that.

30:18.266 --> 30:22.476 align:middle
And your second question
was-what was your

30:22.476 --> 30:23.236 align:middle
second question?

30:23.236 --> 30:24.186 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

30:24.696 --> 30:26.806 align:middle
Yes. It was on what
indicators you're going

30:26.806 --> 30:28.606 align:middle
to be looking at,
financial and real.

30:28.906 --> 30:29.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:30.576 --> 30:32.866 align:middle
Well, I think, in
the very near term,

30:32.866 --> 30:38.206 align:middle
we're going to be
looking at, you know,

30:38.386 --> 30:40.056 align:middle
the Treasury market function.

30:40.196 --> 30:41.466 align:middle
We're really going
to be looking to see

30:41.956 --> 30:44.426 align:middle
that financial markets are
returning to more liquid,

30:44.426 --> 30:45.946 align:middle
more normal functioning.

30:46.556 --> 30:48.486 align:middle
That is an essential
part of our job.

30:48.826 --> 30:50.686 align:middle
That is-actually, you
know, that is the thing

30:50.686 --> 30:53.246 align:middle
that central banks were
originally designed to do,

30:53.246 --> 30:56.866 align:middle
was to provide liquidity to
financial systems in stress,

30:56.976 --> 30:58.566 align:middle
so we take that job
very seriously.

30:58.566 --> 31:01.516 align:middle
It's probably the most important
thing we're doing now is that.

31:01.696 --> 31:03.576 align:middle
So we'll be watching that
and we'll be prepared

31:03.576 --> 31:06.976 align:middle
to use our tools when
and as appropriate

31:07.056 --> 31:10.926 align:middle
to address-to-to support
market functioning and really

31:10.926 --> 31:14.806 align:middle
to support the flow of credit
to households and businesses.

31:14.906 --> 31:15.986 align:middle
Those are the things
I'll be looking at.

31:16.106 --> 31:17.576 align:middle
You know, in terms
of economic data,

31:17.926 --> 31:19.206 align:middle
I think it's just so early.

31:19.206 --> 31:22.456 align:middle
You know, we're really
looking-I mean,

31:22.566 --> 31:26.016 align:middle
the economic data
will follow the data

31:26.016 --> 31:27.206 align:middle
on the spread of the virus.

31:27.516 --> 31:29.556 align:middle
You know, I think what
happens-what happens during the

31:29.556 --> 31:31.986 align:middle
spread of the virus is probably
not going to tell you a lot

31:31.986 --> 31:34.316 align:middle
about where the economy
will be in six months.

31:34.856 --> 31:37.556 align:middle
It's going to be-because you may
have a-you probably have a bad

31:37.556 --> 31:39.556 align:middle
quarter in the second quarter,
but if things get better

31:39.556 --> 31:42.716 align:middle
in the third quarter, that
probably won't matter so much.

31:42.716 --> 31:44.266 align:middle
We may be able to
come back from that.

31:44.266 --> 31:47.866 align:middle
If it lasts longer-so that's
really what I'll be looking for,

31:47.976 --> 31:50.486 align:middle
is-is what is going to be
the spread of the virus

31:50.486 --> 31:51.756 align:middle
and what's the path
it's going to take

31:52.126 --> 31:53.326 align:middle
and what are the
effects on the economy.

31:54.326 --> 31:55.056 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:55.056 --> 31:58.236 align:middle
Okay, we'll go to NBC News now.

31:58.266 --> 31:58.786 align:middle
DAVID GURA.

31:58.946 --> 31:59.676 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

31:59.676 --> 32:01.656 align:middle
This is David Gura
from NBC News.

32:01.656 --> 32:03.936 align:middle
And a lot of good questions
here, so I'll-I'll turn

32:03.936 --> 32:06.276 align:middle
to one that's more
philosophical in nature.

32:06.406 --> 32:08.486 align:middle
And you've talked a bit
about the-the disruptions

32:08.486 --> 32:10.586 align:middle
from the coronavirus outbreak

32:10.586 --> 32:14.186 align:middle
and having lower economic
activity for a period of time.

32:14.186 --> 32:16.246 align:middle
I'm reckoning with fact that my
kid's going to be out of school

32:16.246 --> 32:18.116 align:middle
until April 20 at the earliest,

32:18.116 --> 32:20.826 align:middle
so we're feeling this
very, very acutely.

32:20.826 --> 32:23.006 align:middle
But I wonder if you could
just give us your perspective

32:23.006 --> 32:24.826 align:middle
on the time horizon here and put

32:24.826 --> 32:27.096 align:middle
that into some broader
context-how you look at this

32:27.096 --> 32:29.506 align:middle
and how anomalous this
thing is with which you

32:29.506 --> 32:31.496 align:middle
and your colleagues are
going to have to reckon.

32:31.996 --> 32:33.176 align:middle
That's my first question.

32:33.176 --> 32:34.976 align:middle
The second is, you know,
we've seen a variety

32:34.976 --> 32:36.876 align:middle
of tools here deployed
over these last few weeks,

32:36.876 --> 32:39.856 align:middle
and the market-which I know
is something that you pay

32:40.326 --> 32:42.946 align:middle
at least minimal attention
to-has reacted negatively

32:42.946 --> 32:43.726 align:middle
to some of that.

32:43.796 --> 32:45.076 align:middle
What do you make of that?

32:45.136 --> 32:46.606 align:middle
Going back to the question
that was asked earlier

32:46.606 --> 32:49.526 align:middle
about the toolbox and-and
what's left-what's left

32:49.526 --> 32:51.096 align:middle
in it, rates are low.

32:51.096 --> 32:53.926 align:middle
Does that provoke any
apprehension, any nervousness

32:53.926 --> 32:56.786 align:middle
on your part that there
are fewer tools therein?

32:57.746 --> 32:58.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:58.996 --> 33:05.116 align:middle
So I guess your first question
is, how-how do we think

33:05.116 --> 33:08.306 align:middle
about the time-the
time for this thing?

33:08.346 --> 33:13.346 align:middle
You know-and the truth is, we
just don't know, and, you know,

33:13.346 --> 33:16.306 align:middle
the experts we talk to will
say that they don't know,

33:16.306 --> 33:17.626 align:middle
and that it's, in
fact, unknowable.

33:17.626 --> 33:20.366 align:middle
It's going to depend
on a number of things.

33:20.366 --> 33:24.296 align:middle
First, it's the measures
that we take to, you know,

33:24.776 --> 33:26.666 align:middle
to-with the social distancing,

33:26.666 --> 33:28.726 align:middle
and the-all the things
businesses and school

33:28.726 --> 33:29.666 align:middle
and everyone's doing now.

33:30.086 --> 33:31.886 align:middle
And that will depend,
to some extent,

33:31.886 --> 33:34.516 align:middle
on how compliant people are.

33:35.146 --> 33:38.686 align:middle
You can see that some countries
have had a-have been able to,

33:38.686 --> 33:41.226 align:middle
through aggressive measures

33:41.226 --> 33:43.056 align:middle
and really high compliance-have
been able

33:43.166 --> 33:46.526 align:middle
to bend the curve
and-and do that.

33:46.526 --> 33:48.736 align:middle
And whether we succeed
or-you know,

33:48.856 --> 33:49.866 align:middle
that's really the question.

33:49.866 --> 33:52.996 align:middle
And I wouldn't-I wouldn't,

33:53.116 --> 33:55.186 align:middle
you know-I'll resist
the impulse to guess.

33:55.186 --> 33:56.556 align:middle
I really don't know
what that will be.

33:56.556 --> 34:00.926 align:middle
In terms of the toolbox,
as I mentioned,

34:01.496 --> 34:04.606 align:middle
we think we have plenty
of policy space left,

34:04.686 --> 34:07.466 align:middle
plenty of power left in our
tools, and I would point

34:07.466 --> 34:09.056 align:middle
in particular to
the liquidity tools.

34:09.756 --> 34:12.866 align:middle
That, I think, is-is right
now the most important thing

34:12.866 --> 34:13.416 align:middle
that we're doing.

34:14.056 --> 34:17.086 align:middle
Our interest rates will
matter-cuts will matter now

34:17.186 --> 34:20.106 align:middle
to borrowers who will get
some relief from our cuts,

34:20.156 --> 34:21.266 align:middle
but they'll matter a lot more

34:21.266 --> 34:22.746 align:middle
when the economy
begins to recover.

34:23.376 --> 34:27.606 align:middle
So-but in the forefront now
are the liquidity tools and-and

34:27.606 --> 34:28.626 align:middle
getting that job done well.

34:28.936 --> 34:32.216 align:middle
In addition, as I mentioned,
we-you know, we have room

34:32.216 --> 34:36.926 align:middle
to provide forward guidance,
to do more asset purchases.

34:36.926 --> 34:39.766 align:middle
I think there-there is room for
us to do what we need to do.

34:40.006 --> 34:42.916 align:middle
I-I would just close, though,
by saying that, you know,

34:42.916 --> 34:47.916 align:middle
typically, fiscal policy does
play a major role when-when

34:47.916 --> 34:48.676 align:middle
there are downturns.

34:48.676 --> 34:51.736 align:middle
You saw-you've seen that
in many past downturns,

34:51.826 --> 34:53.986 align:middle
and that's beyond just
the automatic stabilizers.

34:53.986 --> 34:57.436 align:middle
Sometimes fiscal policy comes
in on a discretionary basis,

34:57.436 --> 35:00.756 align:middle
and that-that will probably need
to be the case here as well.

35:01.206 --> 35:04.146 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:04.146 --> 35:07.626 align:middle
Okay, we'll go to Politico.

35:08.266 --> 35:08.726 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

35:08.726 --> 35:10.166 align:middle
Hi, Victoria Guida
with Politico.

35:10.166 --> 35:11.506 align:middle
Thanks for doing the call.

35:11.686 --> 35:14.536 align:middle
I just wanted to ask, do
you see a bigger role here

35:14.536 --> 35:16.566 align:middle
for the Financial
Stability Oversight Council?

35:17.156 --> 35:19.586 align:middle
With all the stress that's
showing up in different markets,

35:19.586 --> 35:22.456 align:middle
do you see risks to
financial stability rising?

35:22.456 --> 35:24.116 align:middle
Do you think there
are some activities

35:24.116 --> 35:26.036 align:middle
that FSOC might want
to crack down on?

35:26.426 --> 35:28.736 align:middle
And are you confident that
there aren't any nonbanks

35:28.736 --> 35:29.956 align:middle
that are systemically important?

35:30.446 --> 35:33.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:33.466 --> 35:36.466 align:middle
FSOC is really something that
the Treasury Secretary chairs.

35:36.986 --> 35:39.356 align:middle
And it's a big group-it's
got a lot of people.

35:39.356 --> 35:40.596 align:middle
I think it serves
a good purpose.

35:41.256 --> 35:43.306 align:middle
But, you know, you should
know that we are, of course,

35:43.306 --> 35:47.326 align:middle
in regular contact with the
other regulatory agencies,

35:47.326 --> 35:50.656 align:middle
constantly talking about
what's going on in markets.

35:50.656 --> 35:52.876 align:middle
There's a lot of
information and conversation

35:52.876 --> 35:54.706 align:middle
on an ongoing basis
there, I would say.

35:55.436 --> 35:59.016 align:middle
So-and the same is true with
our central bank contacts

35:59.016 --> 35:59.636 align:middle
around the world.

35:59.636 --> 36:02.576 align:middle
We're in ongoing contact with
all the major central banks

36:03.096 --> 36:04.726 align:middle
around the world, talking
about what's going on.

36:04.726 --> 36:07.116 align:middle
We're having group calls,
we're calling one-on-one,

36:07.306 --> 36:09.516 align:middle
so there's quite a lot

36:09.516 --> 36:12.096 align:middle
of information being
exchanged there.

36:12.906 --> 36:15.776 align:middle
And then, your question
was, nonbank SIFIs.

36:15.776 --> 36:18.596 align:middle
You know, again,
that's a judgment

36:18.676 --> 36:20.916 align:middle
for the-that's a
judgment for the FSOC.

36:21.696 --> 36:25.746 align:middle
I-you know, I'm comfortable with
the actions that we've taken,

36:25.746 --> 36:27.986 align:middle
and if-if we do identify,
you know,

36:28.536 --> 36:32.016 align:middle
a systemically important
nonbank institution,

36:32.016 --> 36:33.606 align:middle
then we wouldn't
hesitate to designate it,

36:33.656 --> 36:36.476 align:middle
but-although I guess now we're
working more on activities,

36:36.476 --> 36:37.496 align:middle
but we do still have the power

36:37.496 --> 36:41.506 align:middle
to designate institutions,
so- MICHELLE SMITH.

36:41.696 --> 36:43.156 align:middle
Okay- VICTORIA GUIDA.

36:43.156 --> 36:45.126 align:middle
Financial stability
risks-are those rising?

36:45.526 --> 36:48.256 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:48.406 --> 36:52.186 align:middle
I'm sorry, the question
was- VICTORIA GUIDA.

36:52.216 --> 36:55.026 align:middle
Are risks to financial
stability rising,

36:55.026 --> 36:57.056 align:middle
given all the stress that's
happening in financial markets?

36:58.096 --> 36:58.876 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:59.336 --> 36:59.806 align:middle
Well, yes.

36:59.806 --> 37:03.526 align:middle
I would say, you know,
that's-that's something

37:03.816 --> 37:07.726 align:middle
that happens when markets
are as volatile as they are,

37:07.906 --> 37:11.546 align:middle
when you're having these big
market movements while markets

37:11.546 --> 37:14.896 align:middle
are trying to understand
what's going on.

37:14.896 --> 37:18.366 align:middle
They're trying to reach a
view on-in high uncertainty,

37:18.366 --> 37:20.326 align:middle
and that's why you see lots
and lots of volatility.

37:20.926 --> 37:23.046 align:middle
I think there's more risk
to financial stability

37:23.046 --> 37:25.526 align:middle
in an era like-like
that than there is

37:25.526 --> 37:28.886 align:middle
when things are really placid,
and, you know, we're going home

37:28.886 --> 37:31.016 align:middle
at 4 o'clock and kind
of bored over here.

37:31.476 --> 37:32.566 align:middle
That's not the case right now.

37:32.726 --> 37:36.946 align:middle
So, financial stability-I would
say those-those risks do rise

37:36.946 --> 37:39.566 align:middle
at a time of high
volatility, particularly a time

37:39.566 --> 37:43.216 align:middle
where we're presenting-presented
with a challenge that is-is kind

37:43.216 --> 37:46.506 align:middle
of unique, really-it's not
something we've all done before.

37:47.066 --> 37:49.936 align:middle
I also want to add,
though, that, you know,

37:49.936 --> 37:52.346 align:middle
we're strongly focused
on financial stability.

37:52.646 --> 37:53.946 align:middle
We monitor it carefully.

37:54.336 --> 37:57.306 align:middle
We've built a very
resilient financial system.

37:57.836 --> 38:00.596 align:middle
The banks are highly
capitalized, lots of liquidity,

38:00.996 --> 38:04.266 align:middle
much better at understanding
and managing their risks,

38:04.726 --> 38:06.046 align:middle
their resilience to stress.

38:06.566 --> 38:10.736 align:middle
So our financial stability
focus-our focus on an event

38:10.736 --> 38:14.816 align:middle
like this over the last 10 years
will pay dividends, in the sense

38:14.896 --> 38:17.456 align:middle
that you have a much more
resilient financial system

38:18.196 --> 38:19.036 align:middle
than you've had before.

38:19.616 --> 38:21.296 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:21.496 --> 38:23.336 align:middle
Okay, we'll go to Market News.

38:24.676 --> 38:25.396 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

38:25.536 --> 38:29.336 align:middle
Hi, this is Jean Yung.

38:29.336 --> 38:34.626 align:middle
I wanted to ask, a few days ago
the Open Market Desk expanded

38:34.626 --> 38:39.286 align:middle
its term repo operations, but
the take-up was a bit low.

38:39.776 --> 38:43.856 align:middle
Is that-did you have
any takeaway from that?

38:44.066 --> 38:47.656 align:middle
Did that indicate to you that
the repos were not working

38:48.046 --> 38:51.406 align:middle
as you had envisioned it,
or was there something else

38:52.576 --> 38:54.076 align:middle
that you learned from that?

38:54.466 --> 38:56.346 align:middle
Thank you.

38:56.416 --> 38:57.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:58.236 --> 38:58.576 align:middle
Thank you.

38:58.576 --> 39:02.476 align:middle
Yes, so we saw, and
everyone saw,

39:02.826 --> 39:07.846 align:middle
that liquidity had become
very strained in Treasury

39:08.166 --> 39:14.376 align:middle
and MBS markets, and we decided
to offer very large quantities

39:14.466 --> 39:17.016 align:middle
of term and overnight
repo to address that.

39:17.016 --> 39:21.136 align:middle
That makes it easier to finance
the purchase of repos-sorry,

39:21.136 --> 39:23.276 align:middle
of Treasuries and MBS.

39:23.276 --> 39:28.696 align:middle
So we did that, and as you
pointed out, the take-up was not

39:28.866 --> 39:31.126 align:middle
as high as many had expected,

39:31.846 --> 39:35.116 align:middle
and I did-we did learn
something from that.

39:35.116 --> 39:37.726 align:middle
We thought that was worth
doing, and what we learned was

39:37.776 --> 39:41.136 align:middle
that we needed to go direct
here rather than trying

39:41.136 --> 39:43.296 align:middle
to intermediate through
the dealers.

39:43.936 --> 39:47.516 align:middle
And so we realized at that
point that we would need

39:47.516 --> 39:50.296 align:middle
to actually purchase
securities for our portfolio,

39:50.686 --> 39:52.006 align:middle
so we did that on Friday.

39:52.006 --> 39:54.396 align:middle
We bought-the next day,
Friday, we went in and we bought

39:54.396 --> 39:57.976 align:middle
across the curve, and we bought,
I think, $37 billion worth

39:57.976 --> 40:00.726 align:middle
of securities, and then we
saw what happened with that.

40:00.726 --> 40:03.806 align:middle
We saw that market function
improved a little bit,

40:04.206 --> 40:06.566 align:middle
but still it wasn't
what we needed.

40:06.566 --> 40:09.966 align:middle
And that's why we brought
together the full FOMC

40:10.026 --> 40:12.706 align:middle
and had our meeting
this afternoon

40:12.706 --> 40:14.236 align:middle
and announced these
measures this evening,

40:14.796 --> 40:17.396 align:middle
which are strong measures,
with the broad support

40:17.396 --> 40:20.486 align:middle
of the Committee to provide
really substantial amounts

40:20.486 --> 40:21.056 align:middle
of liquidity.

40:21.056 --> 40:23.686 align:middle
And I-I think you
can see us working

40:23.756 --> 40:26.486 align:middle
through different solutions
and-and finding the one

40:26.486 --> 40:27.676 align:middle
that we think really will work

40:27.676 --> 40:29.876 align:middle
and then acting quite
vigorously,

40:29.876 --> 40:33.746 align:middle
quite aggressively
tonight to implement it.

40:34.476 --> 40:34.896 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:34.896 --> 40:37.456 align:middle
Okay, we're going to go to
Reuters for the last question.

40:38.506 --> 40:39.056 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

40:39.056 --> 40:39.746 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

40:39.746 --> 40:40.836 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

40:41.106 --> 40:42.266 align:middle
Two things, if I could.

40:42.266 --> 40:45.186 align:middle
Could you describe any
discussions you've had

40:45.186 --> 40:49.466 align:middle
in coordination with the major
banks-this whole talk of drawing

40:49.466 --> 40:50.566 align:middle
down liquidity, drawing

40:50.566 --> 40:53.606 align:middle
down capital buffers-how
far can they draw it down?

40:53.606 --> 40:57.696 align:middle
How much do you think they can
put out-out to customer finance?

40:57.746 --> 41:00.726 align:middle
Did you get explicit agreements
for them that this will go

41:00.726 --> 41:02.806 align:middle
to customer finance
and not something else?

41:03.376 --> 41:05.756 align:middle
And then, secondly, is
there any discussion

41:05.756 --> 41:09.796 align:middle
yet of-of using the 13(3) powers
of the Fed to do some sort

41:09.796 --> 41:11.396 align:middle
of direct lending facility?

41:12.256 --> 41:12.766 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:12.766 --> 41:16.076 align:middle
We have given broad general
guidance to the banks.

41:16.486 --> 41:20.406 align:middle
We'd like them to use their
buffers to provide-you know,

41:20.716 --> 41:24.606 align:middle
to provide loans and-and also
to work with their borrowers.

41:24.606 --> 41:27.206 align:middle
As you can see, we're providing
a lot of guidance to them

41:27.206 --> 41:30.746 align:middle
across a wide range, and they're
saying they have every intention

41:30.746 --> 41:31.276 align:middle
of doing that.

41:31.596 --> 41:32.506 align:middle
That's good to hear.

41:32.676 --> 41:35.096 align:middle
And-so we'll see
how that works out.

41:35.126 --> 41:38.036 align:middle
There-there are a lot of
discussions going on, and,

41:38.036 --> 41:39.076 align:middle
you know, we hear
the banks saying,

41:39.076 --> 41:41.126 align:middle
that's exactly what
they're going to do,

41:41.126 --> 41:42.506 align:middle
so that's a good thing.

41:43.756 --> 41:46.886 align:middle
You know, we're-of course,
we've-we're-we have nothing

41:46.886 --> 41:50.456 align:middle
to announce on-on 13(3) powers,
but, you know, of course,

41:50.456 --> 41:53.186 align:middle
we-that's part of our
playbook in-you know,

41:53.526 --> 41:55.356 align:middle
in any situation like this.

41:55.356 --> 41:59.206 align:middle
So, as I said, we're-we're
prepared to use our authorities

41:59.926 --> 42:05.676 align:middle
as is appropriate to-you know,
to support borrowing and lending

42:05.676 --> 42:09.466 align:middle
in the economy, and, hence,
to support the availability

42:09.466 --> 42:10.876 align:middle
of credit to households
and businesses.

42:11.776 --> 42:12.676 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:13.316 --> 42:15.946 align:middle
Okay, thank you very much.

